WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.148 --> 00:00:09.092
A storm is coming toward the shore.

2
00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:11.353
Dark clouds fill the horizon.

3
00:00:12.193 --> 00:00:14.834
At first it seems to be a large but ordinary storm.

4
00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:17.675
But the rain falls more heavily than usual.

5
00:00:18.456 --> 00:00:23.038
The wind begins to blow faster and faster, more than one hundred kilometers per hour.

6
00:00:25.979 --> 00:00:30.653
Soon the wind shakes the street signs back and forth, as if an invisible giant were trying to

7
00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:31.794
pull them out of the Earth.

8
00:00:32.635 --> 00:00:33.635
Trees begin to fall.

9
00:00:34.476 --> 00:00:36.698
The force of the wind rips roofs from buildings.

10
00:00:37.518 --> 00:00:39.920
The wind has pushed the ocean waves farther inland.

11
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.564
Suddenly the ocean seems to rise up—as if the sea level were suddenly six meters higher.

12
00:00:49.126 --> 00:00:52.448
The water surges forward and washes away everything that is not secure.

13
00:00:53.169 --> 00:00:58.824
This is a hurricane: a storm that can cover an area 800 kilometers wide and whose clouds can

14
00:00:58.865 --> 00:01:00.525
tower 16 kilometers high.

15
00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:06.868
Also known as cyclones and typhoons, hurricanes are the largest and most damaging storms on Earth.

16
00:01:08.989 --> 00:01:13.491
The word "hurricane" comes from Hurakan, the name of the Caribbean god of storms.

17
00:01:14.351 --> 00:01:17.252
These giant storms develop in different tropical regions.

18
00:01:18.092 --> 00:01:21.574
The storms from the Caribbean and the Gulf of Mexico are called hurricanes.

19
00:01:22.454 --> 00:01:26.496
In India, they are called cyclones, and in the Pacific, they are typhoons.

20
00:01:28.704 --> 00:01:30.725
Hurricanes form in an unusual way.

21
00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:34.248
They do not begin as a single storm that slowly grows.

22
00:01:35.068 --> 00:01:38.090
Near the equator, large thunderstorms tend to rotate.

23
00:01:38.891 --> 00:01:43.614
This movement is caused by the Earth's rotation and the regular direction of the Earth's winds near the

24
00:01:43.674 --> 00:01:51.680
equator. If several tropical thunderstorms are close together, they can merge to form a single giant storm called

25
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:52.260
a hurricane.

26
00:01:53.021 --> 00:01:54.441
Hurricanes always rotate.

27
00:01:55.162 --> 00:01:57.884
Some rotate clockwise, some counterclockwise.

28
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.723
Hurricanes that rotate clockwise are formed south of the equator; hurricanes that rotate counterclockwise are formed north of

29
00:02:05.763 --> 00:02:06.303
the equator.

30
00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:14.687
At first glance, a hurricane may appear to be just a gigantic, swirling storm cloud, but hurricanes have

31
00:02:14.727 --> 00:02:15.967
a complicated structure.

32
00:02:16.828 --> 00:02:20.809
A hurricane's winds rotate faster and faster the closer they get to its center.

33
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.212
But right in the middle is the eye of the hurricane, where there is almost no wind, clouds,

34
00:02:26.252 --> 00:02:26.572
or rain.

35
00:02:27.488 --> 00:02:30.689
The eye of a hurricane can be up to fifty kilometers wide.

36
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:37.052
Immediately surrounding the eye of a hurricane is a towering wall of cloud called the eyewall.

37
00:02:37.852 --> 00:02:43.294
Here, the hurricane's wind speed is fastest, so fast that it can produce tornadoes with winds of almost

38
00:02:43.374 --> 00:02:44.975
five hundred kilometers per hour.

39
00:02:45.695 --> 00:02:50.537
Hurricanes are formed over areas of warm ocean and are fed by the humid air above the water.

40
00:02:52.758 --> 00:02:56.940
As long as the hurricane stays over the warm ocean water, its strength increases.

41
00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:02.346
The warm air rises, generating wind and pulling in more humid air to fuel its storm.

42
00:03:03.126 --> 00:03:06.608
When a hurricane moves over land or cool water, it begins to weaken.

43
00:03:07.368 --> 00:03:11.710
But hurricanes are so strong that it can take up to ten days for one to lose its

44
00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:17.953
power. The World Meteorological Organization is responsible for naming hurricanes.

45
00:03:18.814 --> 00:03:23.756
Each year hurricanes are named in alphabetical order, alternating between masculine and feminine names.

46
00:03:24.656 --> 00:03:27.938
Not all the letters of the alphabet are used to begin the name of a hurricane.

47
00:03:28.758 --> 00:03:33.901
Q, U, X, Y, and Z are omitted, leaving only 21 letters of the alphabet available.

48
00:03:36.103 --> 00:03:40.925
If more than 21 hurricanes form in one year, the letters of the Greek alphabet are used.

49
00:03:41.806 --> 00:03:46.969
The twenty-second hurricane would be named Alpha, the twenty- third would be named Beta, and so on.

50
00:03:47.649 --> 00:03:52.111
When a hurricane has caused a terrible tragedy, a country can request that the name of the hurricane

51
00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:56.037
be retired so that no future hurricane will have that name again.

52
00:03:57.818 --> 00:04:01.920
Just as storms can differ in the speed of their winds and the amount of rain they cause,

53
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:02.921
so can hurricanes.

54
00:04:03.741 --> 00:04:08.303
Hurricanes are rated from categories one to five, category five being the strongest.

55
00:04:09.164 --> 00:04:13.466
The system of classifying hurricanes is called the Saffir-Simpson hurricane scale.

56
00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:18.268
Even a category one or category two hurricane can have a disastrous impact.

57
00:04:20.708 --> 00:04:25.510
A slow-moving hurricane can drop a tremendous amount of rain, causing landslides or floods.

58
00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:31.332
But in general, the higher the category number, the more dangerous the hurricane is to property and people.

59
00:04:32.153 --> 00:04:35.954
The high winds of a hurricane can push the surface of the ocean into a tall wave.

60
00:04:36.855 --> 00:04:40.016
When this powerful wave hits the shore, it is called the storm surge.

61
00:04:42.617 --> 00:04:47.579
Wind speed and the level of the storm surge are the primary characteristics used to classify hurricanes.

62
00:04:48.359 --> 00:04:50.200
Hurricanes are dangerous in many ways.

63
00:04:51.104 --> 00:04:55.285
People can be killed or injured by the flying debris propelled by their powerful winds.

64
00:04:57.626 --> 00:05:03.808
After the hurricane has passed, many may die from disease, since sanitation systems frequently break down during a

65
00:05:03.848 --> 00:05:08.709
hurricane. But 90 percent of the victims of hurricanes are drowned by the storm surge.

66
00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:15.031
Some hurricanes have left 150 kilometers of coastal land submerged underneath 3 meters of water.

67
00:05:16.991 --> 00:05:20.012
Many parts of the world have been repeatedly struck by hurricanes.

68
00:05:20.904 --> 00:05:25.788
With millions of people living in big coastal cities, large populations are at risk.

69
00:05:26.629 --> 00:05:31.573
In response to the threat of hurricanes, governments in these areas have developed evacuation plans.

70
00:05:32.474 --> 00:05:37.418
Unfortunately meteorologists cannot perfectly forecast where and when a hurricane will hit land.

71
00:05:39.299 --> 00:05:44.824
Because it is hard to evacuate whole cities with only a few days' notice, many governments provide hurricane

72
00:05:44.864 --> 00:05:46.385
shelters in vulnerable areas.

73
00:05:47.265 --> 00:05:50.088
Sometimes these are large public buildings, such as stadiums.

74
00:05:51.088 --> 00:05:54.870
Other shelters are specifically designed to be used only during hurricanes.

75
00:05:57.272 --> 00:06:03.355
A powerful hurricane can leave chaos in its wake; bridges can be torn down, electricity and sanitation systems

76
00:06:03.395 --> 00:06:04.176
can be destroyed.

77
00:06:05.016 --> 00:06:09.699
It is recommended that people who live in areas affected by hurricanes store enough food and drinking water

78
00:06:09.739 --> 00:06:10.799
to last several days.

79
00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.621
The food should be canned and nonperishable.

80
00:06:14.421 --> 00:06:18.524
It may be a long time before help can arrive, so it is important to be prepared.

81
00:06:20.828 --> 00:06:25.050
Global warming may make coastal regions even more vulnerable to damage by hurricanes.

82
00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:29.053
Hurricanes gain their strength from warm water in tropical regions.

83
00:06:29.973 --> 00:06:33.035
If global warming increases, so will the temperature of the oceans.

84
00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:40.079
Higher temperatures, it is believed, will lead to more hurricanes than before—and these hurricanes are likely to be

85
00:06:40.179 --> 00:06:40.859
more powerful.
